# Per-person, per-machine CoreHPC settings.  COPY, DO NOT EDIT IN PLACE:
#
#     cp corehpc/chpc.env.local.example chpc.env.local      # at YOUR project root
#     $EDITOR chpc.env.local
#     source corehpc/env.sh
#     chpc-prime.sh                     # one Duo push; opens the session
#     corehpc/lab/bin/chpc-tool doctor  # what software you can actually reach
#
# `chpc.env.local` is gitignored in both this repo and the projects that embed
# it, because it is about you and your laptop, not about the science.
#
# env.sh sources the first of: $CHPC_ENV_LOCAL, <your project>/chpc.env.local,
# corehpc/lab/chpc.env.local -- and sources it BEFORE lab/chpc.env, so set
# inputs here, never derived paths like CHPC_LAB or CHPC_CONTAINERS.
#
# Everything below uses the ${NAME:-value} form so that a value you export in
# the shell still wins. A plain `NAME=value` here would beat your environment
# and beat `sbatch --export`, which is a bug the parent project has been bitten
# by more than once.

# --- who you are -------------------------------------------------------------
# Your UCSF CAMPUS username, which is usually NOT your laptop username. env.sh
# works it out from `ssh -G $CHPC_HOST` when your ~/.ssh/config is set up, and
# falls back to $USER when it cannot -- which is silently wrong, because it
# then builds /mnt/scratch/user/<your-laptop-name>. Set it explicitly and the
# question never arises.
#CHPC_USER="${CHPC_USER:-jdoe}"

# --- how you reach the cluster -----------------------------------------------
# SSH aliases, as defined in your ~/.ssh/config. Install ssh/config.example
# there first and put your campus username in it; docs/01 has the full walk
# through, including that you must ssh-copy-id to the BASTION and the LOGIN
# NODE separately, because they have different home directories.
#CHPC_HOST="${CHPC_HOST:-chpc}"
#CHPC_BASTION="${CHPC_BASTION:-chpc-bastion}"

# --- which share you are in --------------------------------------------------
# The Craik lab FAC share. Change this only if you are in a different lab: it
# derives $CHPC_LAB, $CHPC_GROUP_SCRATCH, the container directory, the software
# directory and the POSIX group name, so one wrong value moves everything.
#FAC_SHARE="${FAC_SHARE:-CX500146_DS1}"

# --- tools -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The conda environment name for the light python layer, when a project does
# not set it itself. Projects normally do.
#CHPC_PYENV="${CHPC_PYENV:-bio}"

# Point the registry somewhere else entirely -- a personal checkout of
# Craik-CoreHPC you are developing a new tool entry in, for instance. Leave it
# unset and you get the one pinned by whichever project you sourced.
#CHPC_TOOLS="${CHPC_TOOLS:-$HOME/src/Craik-CoreHPC/tools}"

# --- Globus: your machine's side of it ---------------------------------------
# Globus is the approved transfer path (docs/02). The cluster side is a site
# fact and already set in lab/chpc.env -- the collection UUIDs, and the fact
# that the scratch collection's "/" is your own $CHPC_USER_SCRATCH. The only
# per-person facts are about the collection running on YOUR machine.
#
# Set up Globus Connect Personal once:
#   1. install it -- https://docs.globus.org/globus-connect-personal/install/
#   2. log in with your UCSF CILogon identity (six digits of your UCID, then
#      @ucsf.edu -- NOT your campus username)
#   3. in its preferences, add the directory you want to stage through and tick
#      it writable. A default install exposes only your home directory, so an
#      external volume will not appear until you add it.
#   4. `globus login`, then `chpc-globus.sh doctor`
#
# Then leave everything below unset unless doctor tells you otherwise. The
# collection id is DETECTED -- `globus endpoint local-id` asks the local GCP
# process -- so there is nothing to paste and nothing to keep in step with a
# reinstall. Declare it only to override that, e.g. to drive a collection on a
# different machine.
#CHPC_GLOBUS_LOCAL_COLLECTION="${CHPC_GLOBUS_LOCAL_COLLECTION:-$(globus endpoint local-id)}"

# Where transfers land on this machine, as an ABSOLUTE local path. It must be
# one of the directories you ticked in step 3. No default: a guess here is a
# directory Globus cannot write, discovered at the end of a transfer.
#CHPC_GLOBUS_STAGING_LOCAL="${CHPC_GLOBUS_STAGING_LOCAL:-$HOME/globus-staging}"

# What "/" means inside YOUR collection, so a local path can be turned into a
# collection path. GCP restricted to your home directory has root "$HOME"; once
# you add an absolute path like /Volumes/... to its config, root is "/". If you
# do not know, leave it: `chpc-globus.sh doctor` resolves the staging directory
# through this value and lists it, which is the only way to find out that does
# not involve a transfer that succeeds into the wrong place.
#CHPC_GLOBUS_LOCAL_ROOT="${CHPC_GLOBUS_LOCAL_ROOT:-/}"
